Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that takes a reference to an std::string containing text in the form of a discount, and returns an std::string containing the same text in HTML format:

```cpp
#include <string>

std::string discountToHtml(const std::string& discountStr) {
    std::string htmlStr;

    // Replace discount characters with corresponding HTML tags
    htmlStr += "<span class='discount'>";
    size_t start = 0;
    while ((start = discountStr.find("%", start)) != std::string::npos) {
        htmlStr += discountStr.substr(start + 1, 1); // Add the percentage symbol
        start += 2; // Skip over the percentage symbol and the next character (usually the discount value)
        htmlStr += "</span><span class='discount-value'>";
        htmlStr += discountStr.substr(start, 1); // Add the discount value
        htmlStr += "</span>";
        start += 1; // Skip over the discount value
    }
    htmlStr += "</span>";

    return htmlStr;
}
```

This implementation assumes that the input discount string follows the format `%value`, where `value` is a single character representing the discount amount (e.g., `%50` for a 50% discount). The function replaces each `%value` with an HTML span element containing two child span elements: one with the percentage symbol, and one with the discount value.

To use this function, simply pass a reference to an std::string containing a discount string as the argument:

```cpp
std::string discountStr = "%25"; // 25% discount
std::string htmlStr = discountToHtml(discountStr);
// htmlStr will now contain "<span class='discount'>%</span><span class='discount-value'>25</span>"
